From af186ceac49fcfe0acb47bfc5ac07c255cadd274 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jayneelatintel <jayneel.vora@intel.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 10:27:47 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 5/9] dist-tp: full-duplex ND sends behind
 LLAMA_DIST_TP_FULL_DUPLEX

dist-comm-nd.cpp had one tx staging buffer and waited for each send to
complete before returning, and dist-comm.cpp ordered every exchange by
rank parity, so a link carried one direction at a time.

With LLAMA_DIST_TP_FULL_DUPLEX=1:
- a 4-slot tx ring (ND_TX_DEPTH) with a post/wait split: nd_post_send
  posts and returns, nd_step reaps completions, and nd_tx_slot_ready is
  the only place that blocks. Sends retire in order, so while fewer than
  ND_TX_DEPTH are outstanding the current slot is free.
- bare headers (ack, bye) get their own slot (nd_post_hdr_only), so they
  cannot overwrite a staged data chunk; drains guard teardown.
- an exchange becomes post -> recv -> drain when the edge's send is on
  ND. TCP edges, and rings that mix ND and TCP edges, keep the parity
  order.

Off by default: without the variable the send path and the exchange
order are unchanged.

Also makes the collective-name filter opt in: it applies only when
LLAMA_DIST_TP_COLLECT_PREFIXES is set, and there is no built-in list.
